{"records":[{"id":"gns2xm2zcx","title":"Tuning the sensitivity of mechanosensory receptors through histidine scanning","description":"Supplemental Movies for the paper titled with \"Tuning the sensitivity of mechanosensory receptors through histidine scanning\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-11-25T09:46:00.494Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gns2xm2zcx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gns2xm2zcx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xiang","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Cancer Immunotherapy","Protein Engineering","Cell Therapy","T Cell Receptor"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gft2rrtd7r","title":"Supplemental materials for study \"Impact of the methotrexate co-prescription on the persistence of TNF inhibitors in psoriasis: a cohort study on the French National Health Data System\"","description":"This supplemental material provides additional methodological details, definitions, and results supporting the main analysis.\n\nData Source: Describes the French national health data system (SNDS), and approvals for study.\n\nMarginal Structural Models (MSMs) with Inverse Probability of Treatment Weighting (IPTW) and Inverse Probability of Censoring Weighting (IPCW): Provides a detailed explanation of the statistical modeling framework used to estimate the causal effect of concomitant methotrexate (MTX) on TNFi persistence, including model specification, weight construction, stabilization, and truncation procedures.\n\nContents\nData source\nMarginal Structural Models (MSMs) with Inverse Probability Treatment Weighting (IPTW) and Inverse Probability Censoring Weighting (IPCW)\nSupplemental Table I. Definition of medicine exposures\nSupplemental Table II. Algorithms to identify comorbidities\nSupplemental Table III. Population baseline characteristics across methotrexate exposure levels\nSupplemental Table IV. Continuous and categorical concomitant methotrexate use across dosing regimes and calculation methods over trimesters\nSupplemental Table V. Impact of concomitant methotrexate on TNFi persistence: sensitivity analyses.\t15\nSupplemental Table VI. Impact of concomitant methotrexate on adalimumab persistence: post-hoc subgroup analysis.\nSupplemental Figure 1. Directed acyclic graph showing the association among biologic persistence, concomitant methotrexate, and time-varying covariates.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-12-15T15:28:29.865Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gft2rrtd7r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gft2rrtd7r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Tran Trong Khoi","last_name":"LE","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tat-Thang","last_name":"VO","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Emilie","last_name":"SBIDIAN","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pharmacoepidemiology","Tumor Necrosis Factor","Longitudinal Analysis","Psoriasis","Methotrexate"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jrtwfg274g","title":"The Supplemental information of the manuscript \"Association of β-Blockers with Methotrexate Efficacy in Psoriasis Patients with Hypertension: A Retrospective Cohort Study\"","description":"This supplemental document supports the study titled \"Association of β-Blockers with Methotrexate Efficacy in Psoriasis Patients with Hypertension: A Retrospective Cohort Study\". It includes comprehensive tables and statistical analyses: Table S1. Baseline characteristics of patients; Table S2. Results of sensitivity analysis","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-12T19:08:42.506Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jrtwfg274g","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jrtwfg274g"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Dawei","last_name":"Huang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rui","last_name":"Ma","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Suyang","last_name":"Lin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jiangluyi","last_name":"Cai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chunyuan","last_name":"Guo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yangfeng","last_name":"Ding","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuling","last_name":"Shi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Dermatology","Beta-Blocker","Clinical Analysis","Psoriasis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4my9tgjr7j","title":"FINANCIAL PERFORMANCE OF BANK — A TAXONOMIC APPROACH BASED ON CAMELS PARAMETER & ITS MAJOR DETERMINANT","description":"This study utilized the ‘CAMELS’ parameter as identified by Narashimam Committee to judge the financial performance of selected 40 banks during the study period 2006 to 2018. Key parameters considered here to judge the financial performance of banks are Capital Adequacy Ratio (CAR), Non-performing Assets (NPA), Return on Assets (ROA), Return on Equity (ROE), Return on Investment (ROI), Cost of Deposits (COD), Net Interest Margin (NIM), Burden-asset ratio (BAR) and Credit-deposit Ratio (CDR). This paper has constructed a performance index of selected banks using Taxonomic approach. Constructed Financial performance index of these banks are regressed on following parameters, namely, bank size (Log assets), Interest income-loan ratio, Non-interest expenses-asset Ratio, equity-asset ratio to judge whether financial performance of banks depend on bank size, operating efficiency, nonbanking performance and solvency of these banks. Results showed that capital adequacy ratio continued to be very high for foreign banks in comparison with private banks. CAR of public sector banks were usually in the lower side. Public Sector banks have more NPA and foreign banks have low level of NPA in India. This study inferred that desperate credit-mobilization drive may be detrimental for financial performance of banks. Panel regression estimate had inferred that financial performance of these banks has improved with the increase in size of banks as well as equity holding of these banks. Expenses other than interest had affected the financial performance adversely though interest incomes of these banks did not have any impact on performance of these banks.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-05-16T16:49:30.174Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4my9tgjr7j","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4my9tgjr7j"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Swati","last_name":"Ghosh","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bishan","last_name":"Sanyal","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Buddhadeb","last_name":"Chandra","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Taxonomic Group","Regression Model"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mhjpvbr7s8","title":"Lysosomal acid lipase regulates cholesterol metabolism during phagosomal maturation","description":"Phagocytosis, a central process in innate immunity, depends on dynamic lipid remodelling, yet how\ncholesterol accumulates on late phagosomes remains unresolved. Here, we identify lysosomal\nacid lipase (LAL) as a key cholesterol ester (CE) hydrolase driving cholesterol mobilization during\nphagosomal maturation. Using integrated lipidomics, chemoproteomics, and biochemical assays,\nwe show that LAL exhibits acidic CE hydrolase activity enriched on late phagosomes, generating\nfree cholesterol essential for lipid raft formation. Pharmacologically inhibiting and genetically\ndepleting LAL disrupts cholesterol-rich lipid raft assembly, impairs phagosomal trafficking, and\nalters pathogen fate – enhancing Staphylococcus aureus persistence, while restricting \nMycobacterium tuberculosis survival. These findings reveal that LAL couples cholesterol\nmetabolism to phagocytosis, defining a mechanistic link between lipid catabolism and antimicrobial\ndefence. By positioning CE hydrolysis as a critical determinant of phagosomal dynamics, our work\nuncovers a metabolic checkpoint in innate immunity and identifies LAL as a potential therapeutic\nnode in infection and inflammation.\n\nMajor findings of the study: Lalistat2/Lysosomal acid lipase knockdown has a pronounced effect on the levels of cholesterol and cholesteryl esters on the phagosomes.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-16T01:57:39.083Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mhjpvbr7s8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mhjpvbr7s8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Siddhesh","last_name":"Kamat","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ojal","last_name":"Saharan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Mass Spectrometry","Lipidomics","Phagocytosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wjhdwcrgct","title":"Tri-glial dysregulation in methotrexate chemotherapy-induced cognitive impairment","description":"Chemotherapy results in a frequent yet poorly understood syndrome of long-term neurological deficits. Neural precursor cell and white matter dysfunction are thought to contribute to this debilitating syndrome. Here, we demonstrate persistent depletion of oligodendrocyte lineage cells in humans who received chemotherapy. Developing a mouse model of methotrexate chemotherapy-induced neurological dysfunction, we find a similar depletion of white matter OPCs, increased but incomplete OPC differentiation and a persistent deficit in myelination. OPCs from chemotherapy-naïve mice similarly exhibit increased differentiation when transplanted into the microenvironment of previously methotrexate-exposed brains, indicating an underlying microenvironmental perturbation. Methotrexate results in persistent activation of microglia and subsequent astrocyte activation that is dependent upon inflammatory microglia. Microglial depletion normalizes oligodendroglial lineage dynamics, myelin microstructure and cognitive behavior after methotrexate chemotherapy. These findings indicate that methotrexate chemotherapy exposure is associated with persistent tri-glial dysregulation and identify inflammatory microglia as a therapeutic target to abrogate chemotherapy-induced neurological dysfunction.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-11-08T00:37:08.238Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wjhdwcrgct","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wjhdwcrgct"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Erin","last_name":"Gibson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Surya","last_name":"Nagaraja","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alfonso","last_name":"Ocampo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lydia","last_name":"Tam","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lauren","last_name":"Wood","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Praveen","last_name":"Pallegar","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jacob","last_name":"Greene","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anna ","last_name":"Geraghty","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andrea","last_name":"Goldstein","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lijun","last_name":"Ni","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pamelyn","last_name":"Woo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shane","last_name":"Liddelow","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hannes","last_name":"Vogel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michelle","last_name":"Monje","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microglia","Chemotherapy","Astrocyte","Oligodendrocyte","Myelination"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ws6vgkd7mh","title":"Synthesized compounds Ernazarova B","description":"N-(-D-galactopyranosyl)-carbamoyl-histidine","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-05-10T10:07:03.161Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ws6vgkd7mh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ws6vgkd7mh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhypargul","last_name":"Abdullaeva","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Chemistry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k27cy7fwh8","title":"Serine catabolism feeds NADH when respiration is impaired","description":"Key raw LCMS data for paper","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-02-23T16:19:13.567Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k27cy7fwh8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k27cy7fwh8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lifeng","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Database"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vfj3r27gnf","title":"Data from Cellular metabolism is a major determinant of HIV-1 reservoir seeding in CD4+ T cells and offers an opportunity to tackle infection","description":"Relative mRNA expression (RT-qPCR) in naive, central memory, transitional memory and effector memory CD4+ T cells from 6 healthy human donors non activated or after three or five days of activation with soluble anti-CD3. Data related to BECN1 expression. Data from failed reactions were removed","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-12-13T21:22:53.988Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vfj3r27gnf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vfj3r27gnf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jose Carlos","last_name":"Valle-Casuso","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mathieu","last_name":"Angin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stevenn","last_name":"Volant","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Caroline","last_name":"Passaes","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Valerie","last_name":"Monceaux","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anastassia","last_name":"Mikhailova","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Katia","last_name":"Bourdic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Véronique","last_name":"Avettand-Fenoel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Faroudy","last_name":"Boufassa","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marc","last_name":"Sitbon","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Olivier","last_name":"Lambotte","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marie Isabelle","last_name":"Thoulouze","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michaela","last_name":"Muller-Trutwin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nicolas","last_name":"Chomont","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Asier","last_name":"Saez-Cirion","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Human Immunodeficiency Virus","T-Helper Cell","T Cell Activation"],"journal_issn":["15504131"]},{"id":"pb2yh64pcj","title":"Serine Catabolism by SHMT2 is Required for Proper Mitochondrial Translation Initiation and Maintenance of Formylmethionyl tRNAs","description":"Raw Data for Serine Catabolism by SHMT2 is Required for Proper Mitochondrial Translation Initiation and Maintenance of Formylmethionyl tRNAs","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-03-02T19:51:18.563Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pb2yh64pcj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pb2yh64pcj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Richard","last_name":"Possemato","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biological Sciences"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]}],"total_records":131281,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":122,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":131281,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":130743,"restricted":64,"embargoed":474},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2187},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1387},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":597},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":452},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":439},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":414},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":406},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":360},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":293},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":259}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":128671},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1901},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":181},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":129},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":73},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":64},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":55},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":41},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":30},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":901},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":827},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":415},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":362}]}}